Done, uncommitted (worker). The _drain_stderr part of this card is done by k25. Dedup: _arm_idle_timer/_clear_idle_timer, touch and the apply_timeouts re-arm now live once in MCP::Hub::Upstream, gated by _idles (an upstream with an idle_timeout attribute: stdio, fork, docker, compose); Stdio's and Docker's copies are gone, Docker::apply_timeouts only forwards request_timeout to its engine. No behaviour change: Http and Perl have no idle_timeout and arm nothing (Http would not want it -- no process to stop); Stdio's dropped "&& pid" guard is implied by state ready. Line overflow: logged once per kill now -- the child is flagged, further reads are dropped and inbuf emptied, so the next child no longer starts on the 16 MB of the old one (before: 2-3 log lines, and the next call was killed too). Tests: t/stdio.t new subtest 'a line over 16MB: the child is killed, once, and the next one starts clean' (red on HEAD: got 2-3 lines, next call "exited (signal 9)"); new t/idle.t (characterization, green on HEAD and after: re-arm on a shorter idle_timeout, 0 stops the countdown, a request re-arms, stopped child arms nothing, a Perl upstream arms nothing); t/docker.t new subtest 'new timeouts reach a running container: its idle timer and its engine'. Green under EV and Poll. Files: lib/MCP/Hub/Upstream.pm, Upstream/Stdio.pm, Upstream/Docker.pm, t/stdio.t, t/idle.t, t/docker.t. Split: w-k39/k26.patch (k25 -> k26). Subject: "Keep one idle timer in the upstream base class, log a line overflow once (k26)". Changes: none. Drift for the main agent: a partial line left in inbuf when a child crashes mid-line still corrupts the next child's first line (reset inbuf in _attach); the idle timer is armed at a call's start, so a call longer than idle_timeout can be stopped mid-call. Drift filed as k122-k127; skill drift as a note on k57.

